# React Native Component Patterns

## Component Structure

Every component follows this order:

1. Imports (see project-conventions)
2. `type` / `interface` for props
3. Named `export function` — no default exports (except where Expo Router requires one — route files)
4. `StyleSheet.create` at the bottom

```tsx
import { Pressable, StyleSheet, Text, View } from 'react-native';

import type { Workout } from '@/db/types';
import { theme } from '@/ui/theme';

type WorkoutCardProps = {
  workout: Workout;
  onPress: (id: string) => void;
};

export function WorkoutCard({ workout, onPress }: WorkoutCardProps) {
  return (
    <Pressable onPress={() => onPress(workout.id)} style={styles.card}>
      <Text style={styles.title}>{workout.title}</Text>
    </Pressable>
  );
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  card: {
    padding: theme.space.s4,
    backgroundColor: theme.color.surface,
    borderRadius: theme.radius.md,
  },
  title: {
    fontSize: theme.font.card,
    color: theme.color.text,
  },
});
```

## Primitives

Use React Native primitives — never DOM elements:

| Instead of HTML      | Use                                              |
| -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| `<div>`              | `<View>`                                         |
| `<span>` / `<p>`     | `<Text>`                                         |
| `<button>`           | `<Pressable>` (or `<TouchableOpacity>` sparingly) |
| `<input>`            | `<TextInput>`                                    |
| `<a>`                | `<Pressable>` with `router.push(...)`            |
| scrollable container | `<ScrollView>` or `<FlatList>`                   |
| page wrapper         | `<SafeAreaView>`                                 |

No `className`, no CSS imports, no `window`, `document`, `navigator.onLine`, or `localStorage`.

## Route vs. Screen vs. Component

- **Routes** live under `app/` (Expo Router). Each file is thin — it imports a screen and default-exports it. Dynamic segments use `[id].tsx`, groups use `(group)/`.
- **Screens** live under `src/screens/` and hold the page-level logic: data hooks, local state, layout, navigation calls.
- **Shared components** live under `src/components/` (feature-specific) and `src/ui/` (theme primitives, charts, error boundary). Shared components are presentational — they receive data and callbacks via props and do not fetch data themselves.

## Navigation

Use Expo Router hooks:

```tsx
import { router, useLocalSearchParams } from 'expo-router';

const { id } = useLocalSearchParams<{ id: string }>();
router.push('/workout/active');
router.back();
```

Do not import from `react-router`, `@react-navigation/native`, or `expo-router`'s internal Link under a different name. Typed routes are enabled via `experiments.typedRoutes` in `app.config.ts`.

## State Management

- **Server state** → React Query hooks from `src/queries/`
- **Auth state** → `useAuth()` from `src/auth/useAuth.ts`
- **Toast** → `useToast()` from `src/ui/ToastContext.tsx`
- **Local UI state** (modals, form inputs, toggles) → `useState` / `useReducer`
- No global state stores.

## Event Handlers

- Name handlers `handleXxx` inside the component; props take `onXxx`
- Always pass `style` as an array or object — don't spread deeply computed styles each render
- For `Pressable`, express pressed state via the `style` function signature rather than `useState`

## UI Clarity

- Every interactive element needs a clear label — icon-only buttons require `accessibilityLabel`
- Use `accessibilityRole` appropriately (`'button'`, `'link'`, `'header'`)
- Empty states should tell the user what the screen is for and suggest the next action
- Avoid stacked modals — prefer inline expansion, bottom sheets, or navigation

## Loading and Errors

- Queries: check `isPending` / `isError` and render a skeleton or compact state; match the approximate shape of the real content to avoid layout shift
- Mutations: surface errors through `useToast()` — never `Alert.alert` for toast-grade feedback
- Forms: local `error` state for inline validation

## Haptics

Import `expo-haptics` and call in meaningful moments:

```ts
import * as Haptics from 'expo-haptics';
void Haptics.impactAsync(Haptics.ImpactFeedbackStyle.Medium).catch(() => {});
```

Always `.catch(() => {})` — haptics are best-effort.

## What NOT to do

- No class components
- No `default export` of components (except route files Expo Router requires)
- No inline styles with hard-coded pixel values — use `theme` tokens
- No direct `supabase.from(...)` calls in a component
- No DOM APIs, window objects, or PWA concepts
